# Opcodes with no arm in src/vm/jit/jit_func.c.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS CHANGED on 2026-08-13. The tier's opcode switch used to end in
# `default: return false`, which declines the WHOLE function -- so an opcode on
# this list evicted every function that so much as MENTIONED it, from a block
# that never ran. It now deoptimises at its own offset instead: the interpreter
# resumes exactly there and the code around it still compiles. An opcode here is
# "slow where I appear", not "I evict my function".
#
# That was worth a benchmark on its own. `OP_GET_EXC` sat on this list, every
# catch block holds one, and so NO function containing a `try` could ever be
# compiled: tests/bench/error_paths' eight-million-iteration counted loop ran
# interpreted end to end -- 124ms against 12ms compiled -- because of a handler
# its own opcode histogram says executes zero times.
#
# So this file is no longer a coverage cliff. It is still a RATCHET, enforced by
# scripts/jit_fusion_check.py, for three reasons that survive the change:
#
#   1. The deopt is not free. It is an unconditional exit from compiled code, so
#      an opcode here on a HOT path costs the whole rest of that body -- and on
#      a loop's straight-line path it costs one compiled entry and one deopt per
#      iteration, which can be worse than never compiling the loop at all.
#      `OP_ELEM_KIND` sitting here by accident cost tests/bench/sort_merge
#      270ms -> 510ms; today it would cost less than that, and still cost.
#   2. Some of them still decline outright. An unarmed opcode inside an INLINED
#      body declines (half an inline cannot be taken back), and so does one at
#      an offset where no deopt site can be built.
#   3. A deopt hands the rest of the function to the interpreter. Arming the
#      opcode is still the fix; this only stops the damage spreading to code
#      that has nothing to do with it.
#
# A NEW opcode with no arm therefore still fails the build, until someone either
# writes the arm or adds it here deliberately, with the reason in the commit.
# Removing a line because an opcode gained an arm is always welcome.
#
# A FUSED opcode may never appear here -- the peephole puts those into exactly
# the hot loops that most want compiling.
OP_NOP
OP_POPN
OP_DUP
OP_DUP2
OP_SWAP
OP_ROT3
OP_SET_UPVALUE
OP_CLOSE_UPVALUE
OP_DEF_GLOBAL
OP_GET_MODULE
OP_ADD_WRAP
OP_SUB_WRAP
OP_MUL_WRAP
OP_POS
OP_IS
OP_IS_NOT
OP_IN
OP_NOT_IN
OP_NOT
OP_CONCAT
OP_JUMP_IF_NULL
OP_FOR_ITER
OP_CALL_KW
OP_CALL_SPREAD
OP_SUPER_INVOKE
OP_CLOSURE
OP_BUILD_DICT
OP_BUILD_SET
OP_BUILD_TUPLE
OP_LIST_APPEND
OP_DICT_INSERT
OP_SET_ADD
OP_SET_SLICE
OP_UNPACK
